  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The figure shows a&nbsp;long coaxial cable in which a current&nbsp;<math><mi>I</mi></math>&nbsp;flows down through the inner cylinder&nbsp;of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math>&nbsp;and the same current flows back up through&nbsp;the&nbsp;outer cylinder of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></math>.&nbsp;The cylinders are insulated from each other and the&nbsp;current is uniformly&nbsp;distributed over the area of the cross-section in each cylinder.&nbsp;The strength of the magnetic field at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;from the axis of the cable is</p>\r\r<p><br />\r<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/3199f0a9-8cb8-41bf-af02-a87a12bea416-image6.jpeg\" /></p>\r",
    "image": null
